<Placemark><name>Murder Rock: Alf Bolin's ambush spot</name><description>Exact spot

Limestone outcrops on Pine Mountain beside the old Springfield–Harrison road, about 10 miles south of Forsyth, Taney County (USGS coordinates). A 1974 account says Highway JJ runs about 60 ft east of the old roadbed, and Coal Pit Hollow spring sits at the foot of the mountain.

Civil War bushwhacker Alf Bolin reportedly hid in these rocks to rob and kill travelers on the road. Look for the old roadbed beside it in the LiDAR.

Access: PRIVATE. Owner proposed public access in 2020; status unknown since. Don't go without permission.

Sources:
KY3: Murder Rock could open to the public (2020): https://www.ky3.com/content/news/Historic-site-Murder-Rock-could-open-to-the-public-landowner-presents-idea-to-county-leaders-567168281.html
White River Valley Hist. Quarterly: Alf Bolin's Buried Treasure (1974): https://sgcld.thelibrary.org/lochist/periodicals/wrv/V5/n6/w74a.html</description><styleUrl>#exact</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-93.159710,36.568320</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Layton's Mill: where Alf Bolin was killed</name><description>Approximate area, 1.6 mi radius

About 3 miles south of Murder Rock, near the Arkansas line. The circle is placed from that distance alone.

An 1862 Union report put Bolin's band in the hills toward 'Laten's Mills'. A disguised Union soldier killed Bolin with a plow colter at the nearby home of Mrs. Foster. His head was later put on a pole in Ozark. Mill and house are long gone.

Access: Unknown. Mostly private land.

Sources:
Ozarks History: Alf Bolin, just facts: https://ozarks-history.blogspot.com/2012/05/alf-bolin-just-facts-part-i.html
White River Valley Hist. Quarterly: Alf Bolin's Buried Treasure (1974): https://sgcld.thelibrary.org/lochist/periodicals/wrv/V5/n6/w74a.html</description><styleUrl>#area</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-93.159700,36.524800</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
