<Placemark><name>Vera Cruz: lost first county seat</name><description>Exact spot

On Bryant Creek, central Douglas County (USGS coordinates).

Douglas County's first seat until 1870, with a courthouse, mills, blacksmith and about 15 homes. Now only a cemetery and an old log church remain. Building pads and the old mill pond may show in LiDAR.

Access: Surrounding land private.

Sources:
Vera Cruz, Missouri (Wikipedia): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vera_Cruz,_Missouri
KSMU (2012): https://www.ksmu.org/local-history/2012-09-27/what-do-you-do-upon-discovering-your-land-was-a-civil-war-battle-site-host-a-reenactment-naturally</description><styleUrl>#exact</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-92.493500,36.913940</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Breadtray Mountain: Spanish silver legend</name><description>Exact spot

Summit near Table Rock Lake, Stone County (USGS coordinates). The legend gives no spot on the mountain.

Centuries-old tale of Spanish explorers sealing a silver mine here. Unproven. Old prospect pits or diggings might show on the slope layer.

Access: Ownership not checked. Confirm before going; no digging on public land.

Sources:
State of the Ozarks: Legend of Breadtray Mountain: https://stateoftheozarks.net/showcase/2018/12/04/the-legend-of-breadtray-mountain/
KY3: Buried treasure in the Ozarks: https://www.ky3.com/2020/12/16/the-place-buried-treasure-in-the-ozarks/</description><styleUrl>#exact</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-93.479220,36.580350</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Alf Bolin's lost grave</name><description>Approximate area, 0.7 mi radius

Right side of the old Swan Creek Road, about 1 mile north and east of Old Forsyth (now Shadowrock Park), Taney County. Circle placed from that description.

After his head was cut off, the outlaw's body was buried in an unmarked grave. The one witness, a boy of 12 at the time, could never pinpoint it later. Look for the old Swan Creek roadbed in the LiDAR.

Access: Mixed private and public land. Unmarked grave: don't dig.

Sources:
White River Valley Hist. Quarterly: Alf Bolin's Buried Treasure (1974): https://sgcld.thelibrary.org/lochist/periodicals/wrv/V5/n6/w74a.html</description><styleUrl>#area</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-93.087800,36.696100</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
