<Placemark><name>Devil's Den: the 'bottomless' sinkhole</name><description>Exact spot

Water-filled sinkhole in an oak grove west of Fordland, Webster County (USGS coordinates).

Said never to have been sounded, with a water level that rises and falls about 30 ft. In the late 1800s it had a ladder, railing and band platform for social gatherings. The owner who bought it in 1980 later stopped allowing visitors.

Access: PRIVATE and closed to visitors.

Sources:
Ozarks Alive: Devil's Den: https://www.ozarksalive.com/stories/ozarkers-visits-to-the-devil-or-more-precisely-to-his-den</description><styleUrl>#exact</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-93.003790,37.159490</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
<Placemark><name>Parson Keithly's hidden gold</name><description>Approximate area, 3.1 mi radius

Near Galena, Stone County. Keithley's Cave near the family land, where he built a rock-walled tomb room. Cave not located; wide circle around Galena.

A preacher came back from the California gold rush with about $6,000 in gold and hid it. His family never found it. Suspects: the cave, or under an apple tree in a far garden.

Access: Unknown.

Sources:
Legends of America: Parson Keithly's Hidden Gold: https://www.legendsofamerica.com/parson-hidden-gold/</description><styleUrl>#area</styleUrl><Point><coordinates>-93.466600,36.805300</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
